d constant demand now I want  to ask you about of course you're here  in Germany about Ukraine's relations  with the EU what is your message to  Western leaders many of them welcomed  the Ukrainian new Ukrainian government  but the coup they're just made about the  reports of corruption and mismanagement  what do you have to say to that that  despite all challenges we're still  moving on the way on the path of our  reforms and we actually delivered a lot  during last period and that was actually  in the report of the European Commission  at the end of the year that we made a  significant progress for instance in the  combating corruption and creation of the  new institutions of course we need some  time to make this institution workable  and to deliver some results of their  work however I still believes that we  are on the right way the EU has backed  visa-free access for Ukraine do you  still see that happening yeah I hope  that we will have this axis this year  where do you see your country with a  current political crisis an economic  crisis where do you see your country in  five years time I suppose that all these  reforms will bring our country closer to  Europe and that we will bring Europe to  our country that we will change the  perception of our citizens and they will  be responsible for their future and will  the conflict in the east of the country  be ended I hope so because this is quite  an artificial conflict and I hope that  we will manage to cope with this  conflict because this is not a matter  the conflict is such this is an issue of  aggression and if we will manage to  block this aggression we will manage to  find a solution inside this society  all right a lot of tasks ahead for your  government deputy foreign minister  Elena's Arkell thank you so much for  joining us this morning in studio thank  you
 

